# MMseqs2 — Apptainer definition, as the MSA stage in front of AlphaFold 3.
#
# This is step 1 of a two-step pipeline. It searches a sequence database for homologues of
# your query and writes one A3M alignment per chain, in the exact shape AlphaFold 3's input
# JSON wants. Step 2 is alphafold3.sif, which does the folding. See README.md in this
# folder for the whole run, end to end.
#
#   apptainer build --fakeroot mmseqs2.sif mmseqs2.def
#   apptainer build --fakeroot --build-arg MMSEQS_BUILD=sse41 mmseqs2-sse41.sif mmseqs2.def
#   apptainer build --fakeroot --build-arg MMSEQS_BUILD=gpu   mmseqs2-gpu.sif   mmseqs2.def
#
# WHY NOT JUST USE ALPHAFOLD 3's OWN DATA PIPELINE? Because it is jackhmmer over ~630 GB of
# databases, which is hours of CPU per target and a storage commitment to match. MMseqs2
# searches the same kind of data in minutes. What you give up is fidelity: these are not
# the alignments DeepMind evaluated the model on. Expect well-covered, well-conserved
# targets to come out much the same, and expect the difference to show on shallow families,
# on orphan sequences and on anything where the MSA was carrying the prediction. If a
# result matters, reproduce it with the official pipeline (`--build-arg AF3_HMMER=yes` on
# the AlphaFold 3 image) before believing it.
#
# MMSEQS_BUILD selects which upstream release binary to install, because MMseqs2 ships one
# tarball per instruction set rather than detecting at run time:
#
#   avx2   (default) Any x86_64 server CPU since about 2014. This is the one you want.
#   sse41            Older x86_64. Noticeably slower; only if avx2 faults with SIGILL.
#   sse2             Very old x86_64. Slower again.
#   gpu              The CUDA build. Fast, and NOT a drop-in: a GPU search needs the target
#                    database converted with `mmseqs makepaddedseqdb` first, and needs an
#                    sm_80 or newer card. Untested here. See %help before reaching for it.
#
# On aarch64 the architecture-specific choice does not apply: the ARM tarball is selected
# automatically (the GPU ARM tarball when MMSEQS_BUILD=gpu), and MMSEQS_BUILD is otherwise
# ignored. The binaries are static, so unlike most images in this repository this one does
# build and run on ARM.
#
# NO DATABASE IS INCLUDED. A useful one is tens to hundreds of GB; it belongs on a
# filesystem, bind-mounted in. `mmseqs databases` inside this image will fetch one for you.

BootStrap: docker
From: python:3.12.12-slim-bookworm

%arguments
    MMSEQS_BUILD=avx2

%labels
    Maintainer  Benedikt Singer
    Tool        MMseqs2
    Upstream    https://github.com/soedinglab/MMseqs2
    Version     18-8cc5c
    Build       {{ MMSEQS_BUILD }}
    Purpose     MSA generation for AlphaFold 3
    License     MIT

%environment
    export PATH=/opt/mmseqs/bin:$PATH
    export PYTHONNOUSERSITE=1

    # MMseqs2 writes a lot of temporary data and respects TMPDIR. Node-local by default;
    # point it at fast local scratch for a real search, never at a network filesystem.
    export MMSEQS_FORCE_MERGE=1

    # The SIF is read-only and $HOME may be unwritable or shared between concurrent jobs.
    export XDG_CACHE_HOME=/tmp/mmseqs-cache

%post
    set -eux
    export DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive

    MMSEQS_BUILD={{ MMSEQS_BUILD }}
    MMSEQS_VERSION=18-8cc5c

    apt-get update
    apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends ca-certificates wget
    rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*

    # One tarball per instruction set. Resolve the ARM case first: on aarch64 there are
    # only two choices, so MMSEQS_BUILD only decides CPU versus GPU there.
    case "$(uname -m)" in
        aarch64|arm64)
            case "${MMSEQS_BUILD}" in
                gpu) ASSET=mmseqs-linux-gpu-arm64.tar.gz ;;
                *)   ASSET=mmseqs-linux-arm64.tar.gz ;;
            esac
            ;;
        x86_64)
            case "${MMSEQS_BUILD}" in
                avx2)  ASSET=mmseqs-linux-avx2.tar.gz ;;
                sse41) ASSET=mmseqs-linux-sse41.tar.gz ;;
                sse2)  ASSET=mmseqs-linux-sse2.tar.gz ;;
                gpu)   ASSET=mmseqs-linux-gpu.tar.gz ;;
                *) echo "MMSEQS_BUILD must be avx2, sse41, sse2 or gpu (got '${MMSEQS_BUILD}')" >&2; exit 2 ;;
            esac
            ;;
        *) echo "no MMseqs2 release binary for $(uname -m)" >&2; exit 2 ;;
    esac

    wget -q -O /tmp/mmseqs.tar.gz \
        "https://github.com/soedinglab/MMseqs2/releases/download/${MMSEQS_VERSION}/${ASSET}"
    tar -xzf /tmp/mmseqs.tar.gz -C /opt
    rm /tmp/mmseqs.tar.gz
    test -x /opt/mmseqs/bin/mmseqs

    # Prove the binary runs on THIS cpu now. A wrong instruction-set choice otherwise
    # surfaces as an "Illegal instruction" core dump inside somebody's job, which looks
    # nothing like a packaging mistake. Note this check passes on the build node and tells
    # you nothing about a different node: if your cluster is heterogeneous, the avx2 build
    # is the one that is safe to assume.
    /opt/mmseqs/bin/mmseqs version

    python -m pip install --no-cache-dir --upgrade pip

    # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    # af3_msa.py — the two helpers, as one module with two entry points.
    #
    # Written flush-left on purpose: an indented heredoc body is still indented Python and
    # raises IndentationError at import, which would only be found by running the image.
    # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    mkdir -p /opt/af3-msa
    cat > /opt/af3-msa/af3_msa.py <<'PYEOF'
"""MMseqs2 -> A3M -> AlphaFold 3 JSON.

Two entry points:

  search   run an MMseqs2 profile search and write one validated A3M per query sequence
  json     write an AlphaFold 3 input JSON that points at those A3M files

The validation is the part that earns its keep. AlphaFold 3 requires of a supplied MSA
that (1) it is A3M, (2) the first sequence is exactly the query, and (3) with all
insertions removed every row is exactly as long as the query. Violate any of those and the
failure appears inside featurisation, well after you have queued the folding job, with an
error that does not name the file. So the A3M is checked here, where it is cheap to fix.
"""

import argparse
import json
import os
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys

MMSEQS = os.environ.get("MMSEQS_BIN", "/opt/mmseqs/bin/mmseqs")


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- FASTA / A3M


def read_fasta(path):
    """Return [(header, sequence)], preserving order. Case and gaps are kept as-is."""
    records = []
    header = None
    chunks = []
    with open(path) as handle:
        for line in handle:
            line = line.rstrip("\n\r")
            if not line or line.startswith("#"):
                continue
            if line.startswith(">"):
                if header is not None:
                    records.append((header, "".join(chunks)))
                header = line[1:].strip()
                chunks = []
            else:
                chunks.append(line.strip())
    if header is not None:
        records.append((header, "".join(chunks)))
    return records


def ungapped_upper(seq):
    """The A3M row as AlphaFold sees it: insertions (lowercase) removed, gaps kept."""
    return "".join(c for c in seq if not c.islower())


def safe_name(header, fallback):
    """A filesystem-safe stem from a FASTA header's first token."""
    token = header.split()[0] if header.split() else fallback
    token = token.replace("|", "_").replace("/", "_").replace(":", "_")
    token = "".join(c for c in token if c.isalnum() or c in "._-")
    return token or fallback


def validate_a3m(path, query_seq, strict=False):
    """Rewrite path in place so it satisfies AlphaFold 3's three A3M rules.

    Returns (kept, dropped). Rows whose ungapped length does not match the query are
    dropped, because a single ragged row makes the whole alignment unusable and one
    bad hit is not worth losing a search over. With strict=True it raises instead.
    """
    records = read_fasta(path)
    if not records:
        raise ValueError("{}: empty alignment".format(path))

    n = len(query_seq)
    out = [(">query", query_seq)]
    kept = 1
    dropped = 0

    # The first record is the query itself in mmseqs output. Compare on the ungapped
    # uppercase form, which is what has to match; then drop it, since out[0] already
    # carries the query verbatim and duplicating it would shift every pairing by a row.
    first_seq = ungapped_upper(records[0][1]).replace("-", "")
    if first_seq != query_seq:
        # Not fatal: it means --skip-query was in play, or the db renamed the query. The
        # query row is prepended either way, so the alignment stays valid.
        print("note: {}: first row is not the query; prepending it".format(path),
              file=sys.stderr)
        start = 0
    else:
        start = 1

    for header, seq in records[start:]:
        row = ungapped_upper(seq)
        if len(row) != n:
            dropped += 1
            if strict:
                raise ValueError(
                    "{}: row {!r} has ungapped length {}, query is {}".format(
                        path, header[:40], len(row), n))
            continue
        out.append((">" + header, seq))
        kept += 1

    with open(path, "w") as handle:
        for header, seq in out:
            handle.write("{}\n{}\n".format(header, seq))
    return kept, dropped


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- search


def run(cmd):
    print("+ " + " ".join(cmd), flush=True)
    subprocess.run(cmd, check=True)


def cmd_search(args):
    if not os.path.isfile(args.query):
        sys.exit("query FASTA not found: {}".format(args.query))
    queries = read_fasta(args.query)
    if not queries:
        sys.exit("no sequences in {}".format(args.query))
    for header, seq in queries:
        bad = set(seq) - set("ACDEFGHIKLMNPQRSTVWYXBZUO")
        if bad:
            sys.exit("{}: not a protein sequence, unexpected characters: {}".format(
                header[:40], "".join(sorted(bad))))

    os.makedirs(args.outdir, exist_ok=True)
    tmp = args.tmpdir or os.path.join(args.outdir, "mmseqs_tmp")
    work = os.path.join(tmp, "work")
    os.makedirs(work, exist_ok=True)

    qdb = os.path.join(work, "qdb")
    resdb = os.path.join(work, "res")
    msadb = os.path.join(work, "msa")
    unpacked = os.path.join(work, "unpacked")

    run([MMSEQS, "createdb", args.query, qdb])

    # -a keeps the alignment backtrace, which result2msa needs; without it the MSA step
    # fails with a message about missing backtraces rather than about the missing flag.
    search = [
        MMSEQS, "search", qdb, args.target, resdb, os.path.join(tmp, "search_tmp"),
        "-a",
        "--num-iterations", str(args.num_iterations),
        "-s", str(args.sensitivity),
        "--max-seqs", str(args.max_seqs),
        "-e", str(args.evalue),
        "--threads", str(args.threads),
    ]
    search += args.mmseqs_args
    run(search)

    # --msa-format-mode 5 is plain A3M. Mode 2 is aligned FASTA (no insertion columns, so
    # it loses information AlphaFold uses), mode 6 adds alignment metadata that AlphaFold
    # does not parse, and modes 0/1 are binary.
    run([MMSEQS, "result2msa", qdb, args.target, resdb, msadb,
         "--msa-format-mode", "5", "--threads", str(args.threads)])

    # Unpack by DB key, then rename from qdb.lookup. Letting mmseqs name files from the
    # accession (--unpack-name-mode 1) breaks on any header with a slash or a pipe in it.
    run([MMSEQS, "unpackdb", msadb, unpacked, "--unpack-name-mode", "0"])

    # qdb.lookup is "<key>\t<header>\t<set>", one line per query, in input order. Read the
    # keys from it rather than assuming key == input index: that happens to be true for a
    # fresh createdb, but it is not a documented guarantee and a silent off-by-one here
    # would attach chain B's alignment to chain A.
    entries = []
    with open(qdb + ".lookup") as handle:
        for line in handle:
            parts = line.rstrip("\n").split("\t")
            if len(parts) >= 2:
                entries.append((parts[0], parts[1]))
    if len(entries) != len(queries):
        sys.exit("createdb made {} entries from {} input sequences".format(
            len(entries), len(queries)))

    written = []
    for index, ((key, db_header), (header, seq)) in enumerate(zip(entries, queries)):
        src = os.path.join(unpacked, key)
        if not os.path.isfile(src):
            sys.exit("mmseqs produced no MSA for query {} ({})".format(key, header[:40]))
        stem = safe_name(db_header or header, "chain{}".format(index))
        dst = os.path.join(args.outdir, stem + ".a3m")
        shutil.move(src, dst)
        kept, dropped = validate_a3m(dst, seq, strict=args.strict)
        print("{}: {} sequences kept, {} dropped as ragged -> {}".format(
            stem, kept, dropped, dst), flush=True)
        if kept <= 1:
            print("WARNING: {} has no homologues at all. AlphaFold will run effectively "
                  "MSA-free for this chain.".format(stem), file=sys.stderr)
        written.append((stem, dst, seq))

    if not args.keep_tmp:
        shutil.rmtree(tmp, ignore_errors=True)
    else:
        print("temporary MMseqs2 databases left in {}".format(tmp))

    print("\nwrote {} alignment(s) to {}".format(len(written), args.outdir))
    print("next: af3-msa-json --fasta {} --msa-dir {} --name NAME --out input.json".format(
        args.query, args.outdir))


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- json


def cmd_json(args):
    queries = read_fasta(args.fasta)
    if not queries:
        sys.exit("no sequences in {}".format(args.fasta))

    if args.ids:
        ids = [i.strip() for i in args.ids.split(",") if i.strip()]
    else:
        # A, B, ... Z, then AA, AB, ... which is the convention AlphaFold's own examples use.
        letters = [chr(c) for c in range(ord("A"), ord("Z") + 1)]
        ids = list(letters)
        for first in letters:
            ids += [first + second for second in letters]
    if args.copies:
        copies = [int(c) for c in args.copies.split(",")]
        if len(copies) != len(queries):
            sys.exit("--copies has {} entries but the FASTA has {} sequences".format(
                len(copies), len(queries)))
    else:
        copies = [1] * len(queries)

    out_dir = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(args.out)) or "."
    sequences = []
    cursor = 0
    for index, (header, seq) in enumerate(queries):
        stem = safe_name(header, "chain{}".format(index))
        chain_ids = ids[cursor:cursor + copies[index]]
        cursor += copies[index]

        chain = {"id": chain_ids, "sequence": seq}

        a3m = os.path.join(args.msa_dir, stem + ".a3m") if args.msa_dir else None
        if a3m and os.path.isfile(a3m):
            if args.inline:
                with open(a3m) as handle:
                    chain["unpairedMsa"] = handle.read()
            else:
                # AlphaFold resolves *MsaPath relative to the JSON file's own directory.
                chain["unpairedMsaPath"] = os.path.relpath(os.path.abspath(a3m), out_dir)
            # unpairedMsa and pairedMsa must BOTH be set or BOTH be null. Setting one and
            # omitting the other is rejected outright, so "" is not optional here.
            chain["pairedMsa"] = ""
        elif args.msa_free:
            chain["unpairedMsa"] = ""
            chain["pairedMsa"] = ""
        elif a3m:
            sys.exit("no alignment for {} at {} (use --msa-free to fold without one)".format(
                stem, a3m))

        chain["templates"] = []
        sequences.append({"protein": chain})

    document = {
        "name": args.name,
        "dialect": "alphafold3",
        "version": 2,
        "modelSeeds": [int(s) for s in args.seeds.split(",")],
        "sequences": sequences,
    }

    with open(args.out, "w") as handle:
        json.dump(document, handle, indent=2)
        handle.write("\n")
    print("wrote {} ({} chain entries, {} chain ids)".format(
        args.out, len(sequences), cursor))
    print("\nFold it with, NOTE both flags:")
    print("  alphafold3 --json_path={} --model_dir=/models --output_dir=out \\".format(
        args.out))
    print("      --norun_data_pipeline --resolve_msa_overlaps=false")


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- main


def main(argv=None):
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="af3-msa",
        description="MMseqs2 MSA generation for AlphaFold 3 (see `af3-msa <cmd> -h`)")
    sub = parser.add_subparsers(dest="command", required=True)

    s = sub.add_parser("search", help="search a database and write validated A3M files")
    s.add_argument("query", help="query FASTA (one record per chain)")
    s.add_argument("target", help="MMseqs2 target database prefix (not a FASTA)")
    s.add_argument("outdir", help="directory for the .a3m files")
    s.add_argument("--threads", type=int, default=len(os.sched_getaffinity(0)),
                   help="default: the CPUs this process is allowed to use")
    s.add_argument("--num-iterations", type=int, default=3, dest="num_iterations",
                   help="profile search iterations (default 3; 1 is a plain search)")
    s.add_argument("--sensitivity", type=float, default=8.0,
                   help="mmseqs -s (default 8.0, i.e. sensitive and slow)")
    s.add_argument("--max-seqs", type=int, default=10000, dest="max_seqs",
                   help="hits kept per query per iteration (default 10000)")
    s.add_argument("--evalue", type=float, default=0.1,
                   help="inclusion e-value (default 0.1)")
    s.add_argument("--tmpdir", default=None,
                   help="scratch for MMseqs2 (default: <outdir>/mmseqs_tmp). Put this on "
                        "node-local disk; MMseqs2 is hard on a network filesystem.")
    s.add_argument("--keep-tmp", action="store_true", dest="keep_tmp",
                   help="keep the intermediate MMseqs2 databases")
    s.add_argument("--strict", action="store_true",
                   help="fail on a ragged alignment row instead of dropping it")
    s.add_argument("mmseqs_args", nargs="*",
                   help="extra arguments passed straight to `mmseqs search`")
    s.set_defaults(func=cmd_search)

    j = sub.add_parser("json", help="write an AlphaFold 3 input JSON")
    j.add_argument("--fasta", required=True, help="the same query FASTA used for the search")
    j.add_argument("--msa-dir", default=None, dest="msa_dir",
                   help="directory holding <stem>.a3m, as written by `search`")
    j.add_argument("--name", required=True, help="job name recorded in the JSON")
    j.add_argument("--out", required=True, help="output JSON path")
    j.add_argument("--seeds", default="1", help="comma-separated model seeds (default 1)")
    j.add_argument("--ids", default=None,
                   help="comma-separated chain ids (default A, B, C, ...)")
    j.add_argument("--copies", default=None,
                   help="comma-separated copy count per FASTA record, for homomers")
    j.add_argument("--inline", action="store_true",
                   help="embed the A3M in the JSON instead of referencing it by path. "
                        "Makes one self-contained file; also makes a large one.")
    j.add_argument("--msa-free", action="store_true", dest="msa_free",
                   help="for chains with no alignment, fold MSA-free instead of failing")
    j.set_defaults(func=cmd_json)

    args = parser.parse_args(argv)
    return args.func(args)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
PYEOF

    python -m py_compile /opt/af3-msa/af3_msa.py

    cat > /usr/local/bin/af3-msa-search <<'SHEOF'
#!/bin/sh
exec python3 /opt/af3-msa/af3_msa.py search "$@"
SHEOF
    cat > /usr/local/bin/af3-msa-json <<'SHEOF'
#!/bin/sh
exec python3 /opt/af3-msa/af3_msa.py json "$@"
SHEOF
    cat > /usr/local/bin/af3-msa <<'SHEOF'
#!/bin/sh
exec python3 /opt/af3-msa/af3_msa.py "$@"
SHEOF
    chmod 755 /usr/local/bin/af3-msa-search /usr/local/bin/af3-msa-json /usr/local/bin/af3-msa

%test
    set -eu
    export PATH=/opt/mmseqs/bin:$PATH
    mmseqs version
    af3-msa --help > /dev/null
    af3-msa-search --help > /dev/null
    af3-msa-json --help > /dev/null

    # A real end-to-end run on a toy database, so the test exercises createdb, search,
    # result2msa, unpackdb, the rename from the .lookup file, the A3M validator and the
    # JSON writer. No network and no GPU, so it runs anywhere the image builds.
    T="$(mktemp -d)"
    cat > "$T/query.fasta" <<'FAEOF'
>chainA test query

FAEOF
    # The "database" is the query plus two near-identical variants: enough for result2msa
    # to produce a genuine multi-row A3M, and small enough to search in a second.
    cat > "$T/target.fasta" <<'FAEOF'
>hit1

>hit2

>hit3

FAEOF
    mmseqs createdb "$T/target.fasta" "$T/targetdb" > /dev/null
    af3-msa-search --threads 2 --num-iterations 1 --sensitivity 5.7 \
        "$T/query.fasta" "$T/targetdb" "$T/msas"
    test -s "$T/msas/chainA.a3m"
    af3-msa-json --fasta "$T/query.fasta" --msa-dir "$T/msas" \
        --name selftest --out "$T/input.json"

    # Assert the JSON is the shape AlphaFold 3 accepts, and the A3M obeys its three rules.
    python3 - "$T" <<'PYEOF'
import json
import sys

root = sys.argv[1]
with open(root + "/input.json") as handle:
    doc = json.load(handle)

assert doc["dialect"] == "alphafold3" and doc["version"] == 2, doc
chain = doc["sequences"][0]["protein"]
assert chain["pairedMsa"] == "", "pairedMsa must be present and empty"
assert "unpairedMsaPath" in chain, chain.keys()
assert chain["templates"] == []

query = chain["sequence"]
rows = []
header = None
with open(root + "/msas/chainA.a3m") as handle:
    for line in handle:
        line = line.strip()
        if line.startswith(">"):
            header = line
        elif line and header is not None:
            rows.append(line)

assert rows, "alignment is empty"
assert rows[0] == query, "first row is not the query"
for row in rows:
    plain = "".join(c for c in row if not c.islower())
    assert len(plain) == len(query), (len(plain), len(query))
print("a3m rows:", len(rows), "| json and a3m both valid")
PYEOF
    rm -rf "$T"
    echo "MMSEQS2_TEST_OK"

%runscript
    exec /opt/mmseqs/bin/mmseqs "$@"

%help
    MMseqs2 18-8cc5c, packaged as the MSA stage for AlphaFold 3.

    THE TWO-STEP RUN (full worked example in README.md):

      # 1. get a database, once. ~60 GB for UniRef90, and it takes a while.
      apptainer exec -B /scratch/db:/db mmseqs2.sif \
          mmseqs databases UniRef90 /db/uniref90 /db/tmp --threads 16

      # 2. search, and write the JSON
      apptainer exec -B /scratch/db:/db:ro -B "$PWD":/data mmseqs2.sif \
          af3-msa-search --threads 32 /data/query.fasta /db/uniref90 /data/msas
      apptainer exec -B "$PWD":/data mmseqs2.sif \
          af3-msa-json --fasta /data/query.fasta --msa-dir /data/msas \
              --name my_complex --out /data/input.json

      # 3. fold, with the OTHER image
      apptainer run --nv -B /models:/models:ro -B "$PWD":/data alphafold3.sif \
          --json_path=/data/input.json --model_dir=/models --output_dir=/data/out \
          --norun_data_pipeline --resolve_msa_overlaps=false

    `--resolve_msa_overlaps=false` is not optional when you bring your own MSA. Without it
    AlphaFold deduplicates your unpaired MSA against the paired one, and since pairedMsa
    is "" but still gets the query added, that can reorder or drop rows.

    One FASTA record per chain. af3-msa-search names each alignment after the record's
    first token, and af3-msa-json looks the files up by the same name — so keep the FASTA
    the same between the two commands. For a homomer, give the sequence once and pass
    --copies: `--copies 2` folds it as two chains sharing one alignment.

    Ligands, ions, nucleic acids and modifications are not written by af3-msa-json. Add
    those entries to the JSON by hand afterwards; the protein chains are the part that
    needs the alignment.

    WHAT THE SEARCH DEFAULTS MEAN

      --num-iterations 3   An iterative profile search: hits from round N seed round N+1.
                           This is what makes MMseqs2 competitive with hhblits on remote
                           homologues. Set 1 for a fast, shallow search.
      --sensitivity 8.0    mmseqs -s. 8.0 is near the top of the useful range; 5.7 is the
                           mmseqs default and perhaps 5x faster.
      --max-seqs 10000     Hits kept per round. AlphaFold uses a few thousand rows at most.
      --evalue 0.1         Permissive on purpose: an MSA tolerates a few false positives
                           better than it tolerates being shallow.

    Anything after the three positional arguments is passed straight to `mmseqs search`,
    so site-specific flags need no change here:

      af3-msa-search q.fasta /db/uniref90 msas -- --db-load-mode 2 --split-memory-limit 100G

    ABOUT THE GPU BUILD (MMSEQS_BUILD=gpu, untested here)

    It is not a drop-in. The target database has to be rebuilt in padded form:

      mmseqs makepaddedseqdb /db/uniref90 /db/uniref90_gpu

    then search it with `--gpu 1`, which also wants `--db-load-mode 2`. It needs an sm_80
    or newer card, and the whole padded database resident in GPU memory or streamed. For
    one-off jobs the CPU build is simpler and quick enough; for a sweep of hundreds of
    targets the GPU build is where the time goes back.

    FIDELITY, STATED PLAINLY: these are not the alignments AlphaFold 3 was evaluated with.
    The official pipeline is jackhmmer over ~630 GB of genetic databases. Build the
    AlphaFold 3 image with --build-arg AF3_HMMER=yes and stage those databases if a result
    has to be defensible.

